title start
kind: research
tags: business-plan brand brand-colour-palette-monochrome-white-red-purple-rules
Brand colour palette monochrome white red purple rules
title end

section start
title start
Abstract
title end

paragraph start
Question: what contrast and colour rules keep a white, red and purple palette readable on a dark monochrome brand. Method: the web accessibility contrast standard, read on 2026-10-29. Result: the standard sets a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5 to 1 for normal text and 3 to 1 for large text, and exempts logotypes. Limit: the standard was written for web pages, not social video, so applying it to overlays is a design choice.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Introduction
title end

paragraph start
The palette is monochrome with white as the main accent and red or purple only when needed. This page sets where each colour is allowed and how readable text must be.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Methods
title end

paragraph start
Searched the web for the contrast threshold of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines version 2.2 and read the W3C understanding page and a WebAIM article. The psychology evidence lives in the research area and is not repeated here.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Results
title end

paragraph start
Threshold checked 2026-10-29: normal text needs a contrast ratio of at least 4.5 to 1 against its background, and large text at least 3 to 1, where large means 18 point or 14 point bold (W3C, success criterion 1.4.3).
paragraph end

paragraph start
Text that is part of a logo or brand name has no minimum contrast requirement (WebAIM, secondary source).
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Discussion
title end

paragraph start
Palette rules for the plan: the background is near black, text and the main accent are white, red and purple are used one at a time and each for one stated reason, and every text and background pair is measured with a contrast checker before use.
paragraph end

paragraph start
The thresholds come from a web standard and are used here as a readability target for video overlays and thumbnails. That use is a design choice and was not tested on viewers.
paragraph end

